<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">27250809</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2017</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>29</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>27</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1550-9397</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>12</Volume><Issue>6</Issue><PubDate><Year>2016</Year><Month>Jun</Month><Day>15</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Journal of clinical sleep medicine : JCSM : official publication of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine</Title><ISOAbbreviation>J Clin Sleep Med</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Recommended Amount of Sleep for Pediatric Populations: A Consensus Statement of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>785</StartPage><EndPage>786</EndPage><MedlinePgn>785-6</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5664/jcsm.5866</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Sleep is essential for optimal health in children and adolescents. Members of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine developed consensus recommendations for the amount of sleep needed to promote optimal health in children and adolescents using a modified RAND Appropriateness Method. The recommendations are summarized here. A manuscript detailing the conference proceedings and the evidence supporting these recommendations will be published in the Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2016 American Academy of Sleep Medicine.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Paruthi</LastName><ForeName>Shalini</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Saint Louis University, St. Louis, MO.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Brooks</LastName><ForeName>Lee J</ForeName><Initials>LJ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Ch